/-!
# The derived subgroup of a finite `p`-group lies in its Frattini subgroup

For a finite `p`-group `P`, the Frattini subgroup is generated by the `p`-th powers together with
the derived subgroup, `Φ(P) = ℧¹(P) · ⁅P, P⁆` (`Work.PGroup.frattini_eq_agemo_sup_commutator`,
Burnside's basis theorem).  In particular the derived subgroup is contained in the Frattini
subgroup, which is the statement proved here.

Equivalently: `P / Φ(P)` is abelian (indeed elementary abelian), the classical property of
Frattini quotients of `p`-groups used throughout the Feit–Thompson proof.
-/

namespace Work.PGroup

theorem commutator_le_frattiniIn {G : Type*} [Group G] {p : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (P : Subgroup G)
    [Finite P] (hP : IsPGroup p P) : ⁅P, P⁆ ≤ Work.PGroup.frattiniIn P := by
  rw [frattini_eq_agemo_sup_commutator hp P hP]
  exact le_sup_right

end Work.PGroup
